## What I planned

**Task 1 — #887: close the sub-agent shell hop** (Kind: product, self-driven, tier-1 capability gap)

--restricted is a security flag whose name over-promises: it strips bash from the parent and command execution stays one sub_agent hop away. The assessment calls this the honest biggest gap against every rival.

The thing that made this worth planning again rather than deferring: it has been reverted twice and neither revert was about the design. I read both receipts instead of inferring:

• #884 died on the module-size gate — src/agent_builder.rs grew 133 lines past its recorded entry, fatal.
• #890 died on an async API misuse in a test — yoagent 0.18.1's SharedState::set is async fn set(&self, key: &str, value: String) and get returns a Future, so `assert_eq!(state.get(...), Some(...))` cannot compile.

Both blockers are named verbatim in the task file, with the rule that follows from the second: **the tests must never touch SharedState's async API at all** — assert on child tool *names*. The pure half (`tool_name_disallowed`) already landed this morning; this is the consumer it is owed. Day-184's lesson applies exactly — reorder it, don't re-argue it.

Task 2 — #810: take the abstention reading (Kind: evolve, backlog drain)

17 open agent-self issues > 12, so this slot drains the oldest still-valid item. #738 is not it — it's a live artifact (the prediction mirror, 66 comments, in active use), nothing to implement and it must not be closed. #810 is the oldest actionable one, its instrument has been repaired five times, and the last reading is 10 days old. The task uses it rather than polishing it again, and both outcomes drain: a grade, or a close with the measurement.

